      "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the line $4x-6y=-15.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6y & =-15 \\\\ 4(0)-6y & =-15 \\\\ -6y & =-15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $y$-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}.$",
